Brock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.




We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been recognized as a Top Employer in Hamilton-Niagara for seven consecutive years. We have been ranked #3 as Canada’s Best Employers and top 10 as one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity. For 2025, Brock has been proudly recognized as one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture, ranked seventh by Forbes in partnership with Statista. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities.




Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock.




Post End Date:

August 23, 2026 at 11:59 PM

This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Administrative Professional (Employee Group)




About the Role:

Reporting to the Head of Library Systems & Technologies, the Library Systems Analyst advances, configures, and integrates Library enterprise solutions using systems analysis design, implementation, development, software solution support skills, and understanding of Library requirements. You will enhance and streamline library systems, provide data and analytic solutions, configure library systems and business processes, and develop integrations into existing and third-party systems to support the library’s strategic goals. You will also be responsible for resolving complex problems and providing comprehensive recommendations and solutions.




The Library Systems Analyst will:

Oversee the day-to-day operation of the technical aspects of the Library Services Platform (i.e., configuration, maintenance, troubleshooting, training, and resolving issues);
Configure library systems and associated processes to enhance and streamline systems;
Provide development specifications for library systems and integrate them with existing University systems;
Provide maintenance support for library systems and web development;
Identify library system problems and work with developers and end users to resolve them;
Implement library application solutions that are reliable and secure;
Test and provide quality assurance measures with developers and stakeholders;
Analyze and implement robust data management procedures by integrating third-party software solutions;
Complete all work tickets, use tracking software to provide a maintenance record for all changes, and share information with colleagues;
Serve as the lead analyst for small projects and enhancements of library system solutions;
Ensure adherence to developmental policies, procedures, and standards;
Ensure usability and acceptance of software applications and systems by developing a strong understanding of customer practices;
Maintain a broad knowledge of state-of-the-art technology, software, and development methodologies.




Key Skills and Experience:

A Bachelor’s degree or college diploma in a related field and experience with analysis and support of library IT business processes;
Familiarity with the services and business practices of academic libraries;
Experience administering library enterprise software (preference for modern library services platforms);
Knowledge of current and emerging library systems (i.e., MARC, BIBFRAME, OpenURL, XML, Linked Data and RDF, OAI-PMH, SIP2 and Z39.50);
Experience querying, manipulating and transforming large quantities of library data using APIs, scripting, and specialized applications;
Understanding of object oriented and database programming languages (i.e., SQL, Web Services, XML, XSLT, Java, PHP, Python, Model-View-Controller) and other integration technologies;
Strong understanding of web scripting technologies (i.e., HTML, CSS, and Javascript);
Strong communication and interpersonal skills;
Proven ability to negotiate solutions and effectively implement them; and
Strong analysis, troubleshooting, and problem-solving capabilities.




Preferred or Asset Skills:

Experience administering enterprise library services platforms;
Experience with ExLibris, Omeka, RESTful Web APIs using XML or JSON, Docker;
Experience troubleshooting in Linux and Windowsenvironments;
Understanding of accessibility requirements (AODA);
Understanding of software development life cycle methodologies;
Experience with financial, human resources, and Student Information software;
Demonstrated ability to comprehend large and complex systems;
Relevant courses in project management, enterprise or business, or information architecture; and
Relevant courses in current development tools.




Salary and Total Rewards:

Job Grade K, Salary Scale ($62,115-99,384);
Target Hiring Salary: $62,500*
This is a permanent position that includes:
Health & Dental Benefits: Comprehensive extended health, dental, and vision coverage.
Pension Plan: Enrollment eligibility in the Brock University Pension Plan.
Vacation: Up to 3 weeks per year, in addition to university holidays.
Flexible Work Arrangements: Hybrid work schedule available.
Professional Development: Eligibility for the Brock Tuition Waiver Program.




*The final salary is based on experience, internal equity, and budget considerations within the target hiring range.




We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy. As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.




Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups.




We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodate the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code. Please contact talent@brocku.ca if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure your participation needs are met.




Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.




We appreciate all applications received. Candidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.




